WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.080
The trouble with a lot of
real world experiments

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:03.760
is that you can't have a control group.

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:06.880
If you're researching, say,
how riverbanks are affected by erosion,

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:08.920
then either you have to
observe the erosion happening

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:13.240
in the wild, with lots of messy variables
like weather and climate and wildlife,

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:16.880
or you have to rely on tiny scale models
or computer simulations.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:20.600
You can't take an actual river,
let it erode its banks for a while,

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:21.440
then stop the water,

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:24.920
rebuild everything, and just run it again
with one thing changed.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:27.960
Unless you build your own river,
which is exactly what

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.360
the University of Sherbrooke in Canada
has done.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:33.240
Will you start the pumps, please!

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:39.360
- So I’ve done a lot of studies
in nature, on real rivers.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.680
You do get excellent information,

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:45.240
but you can't pick out
specific variables and play with them.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:49.080
We built this with the idea
that we can study sediment transport

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:53.160
on a scale that represents
close to a small river or creek.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:56.080
The water starts in the reservoir.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:59.840
The pumps pump from the reservoir,
and then it discharges into the

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.400
approach channel,
which is made to be very large,

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:04.960
so that we diminish any turbulence

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:07.040
that might be brought in there
by the pumps.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:12.000
After that, we have a weir, and then it
arrives in the river and goes down.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.760
And that's where we do our experiments.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:17.520
And the river falls
over an overflow into a sediment trap.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.320
From the sediment trap,
it goes back into the reservoir.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.600
It’s a recirculating system.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.840
Probably takes 2 or 3 minutes
for the water to recirculate.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.240
It is all repeatable.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:28.320
We can adjust the pumps
for a certain level,

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.600
have that one day, come back a week later

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.920
and have the same flow
coming through the system.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:34.760
We have two 70kW pumps.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:39.480
At the moment, we have 800 liters per
second, and we have room for another pump.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:41.880
- Okay, compared to the Mississippi,
this doesn't look that big.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.080
But here's how powerful it is.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:46.080
We anchored down a GoPro camera
to get those shots

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:49.600
near the weir,
and the water just blasted it away.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:51.520
One of the team here
had to wade into save it

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.920
before it got washed into the basin,
never to be recovered.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:55.320
“Thank you, Basim!”

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:58.720
So it does a very good job of
representing small rivers.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:02.360
And the processes that happen
here are much more related to,

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:05.360
say, a river than small laboratories.

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:09.000
Obviously, in larger rivers
you have more scales of turbulence.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.080
But those scales,
they should be similar in our size.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:17.400
- I've seen a small scale model years ago,
when I visited the Bay Model in California.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.800
It's a 1:1000 scale
copy of the San Francisco Bay,

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.200
which means it's the size
of a football field.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:25.520
But the 1960s engineers who built
it had to stick a load of copper strips

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:28.800
into the base to make the water behave
like it would at full scale.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.640
By all accounts, they did really well,
particularly for before computers, but

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:36.280
it's no comparison to having a model
that is closer to real-world size.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:40.360
- This summer we were looking at erosion
induced by a bank,

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:41.880
an artificial bar we put in.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:44.080
Throughout the world
there's a problem in understanding

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.680
how much erosion is going to happen
for a given flood.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:49.680
So as an engineer,
you want to be able to run a model

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:52.960
that will tell you how much bank erosion
you'll have in the future.

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:57.200
We would also, next year,
like to start looking at ice cover

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:01.200
and the effect of ice on things
like bank erosion and floods.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:04.120
Because ice related flooding
is a huge problem.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:08.600
Having a river where you can model
ice would be quite unique.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:11.200
- A lot of this work can be done
with computer simulations now,

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:15.440
but the code for those simulations
still has to be validated and checked

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:16.440
somewhere like this.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:17.880
And sometimes the researchers here

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:21.240
are working on things that there is
no computer model for, which surprised me.

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:24.720
I assumed that simulating water
was pretty much a solved problem, but no.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.240
There are still new technologies
that need to be tested,

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.960
and one of them is up there
getting the aerial shots for this video.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:34.200
- To model this river
with a particle interaction model

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.040
would take many days,
months, years to run.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:41.000
We have projects involving large scale
particle image velicometry.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:44.640
This is a new technique with a drone,
you'll take the water surface

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:48.200
and then you can have a velocity,
which you can then relate to a discharge:

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:52.560
how much water is moving through a section
of river per second.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:56.640
And having a method of measuring discharge
without actually going into the river is

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:00.400
very useful, because it's very difficult
to go into these rivers at high flows.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:01.560
It’s very dangerous, in fact.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:05.200
The water surface can be affected
just by the general wind,

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:09.040
but then that can influence
or bias the results of the flow

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:10.480
we actually want to measure.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:14.720
- And sure, by the grand scales of
waterparks and white water rafting centres,

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:16.640
this isn't the biggest
artificial river in the world,

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:19.600
but it is one that's built for research.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:21.480
Even if that does mean that
the team here wouldn't let me

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:23.800
bring my own inflatable tube
to ride down it.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:27.240
Nobody has ever been down
on an inflatable tube.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:31.320
It's an interesting idea,
but I don't think I would advise it.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:32.640
I don't think I would advise it!

